WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.310
PRIYANKA: Hi, I'm
Priyanka [INAUDIBLE],,

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.830
and this is AI
Simplified, where we

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.050
will take the journey
from data sets

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.230
all the way to deployed
machine learning models.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.330
No matter which service
you offer today,

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.210
it is crucial to use the data
you have to make predictions

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:19.780
so you can improve your apps
and the user experience.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.470
But most teams have
varying levels of machine

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.880
learning expertise, ranging from
novice all the way to experts.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.360
To accelerate AI innovation,
you need a platform

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.360
that can help you build
expertise for those novice

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.200
users, and provide a seamless
and flexible environment

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.000
for those experts.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.270
This is where
Vertex AI comes in.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.630
It provides tools for every
step of the machine learning

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.270
workflow across
different model types,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.810
for varying levels of
machine learning expertise.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.670
Before we take a
look at Vertex AI,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.250
though, let's understand
the typical machine

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.080
learning workflow.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.740
After defining your prediction
task, the first thing you do

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.420
is ingest the data, analyze
it, and then transform it.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.240
Then you create and
train the model.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.170
Evaluate that model for
efficiency and optimization.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.870
And then deploy it
to make predictions.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.110
Now, with Vertex AI, you get
a simplified machine learning

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.900
workflow in one central place.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.140
Ingestion, analysis,
and transforming

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.930
is really all about
data preparation.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:32.970
And you do that using managed
data sets within Vertex AI.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.010
You have tools to
create the data set

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:39.450
by importing your data using
the console, or just the API.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.720
You can also label and
annotate the data right

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.370
from within the console.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.310
For model training,
you have two options--

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.950
AutoML or custom.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.950
With varying machine learning
expertise on the team,

00:01:52.950 --> 00:02:01.740
for some use cases such as
images or videos, text files,

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.010
and tabular data,
AutoML works great.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.850
With AutoML, you don't need to
write any of the model code.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.949
Vertex AI will take
care of finding

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:13.330
the best model for that task.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.240
And for other use
cases where you

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.170
would like more control over
your model's architecture,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.970
use custom models.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.690
Now, custom models are great
for frameworks and architectures

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.830
and code that you want
to write yourself.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:30.300
So this works great for
TensorFlow or PyTorch code.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.240
Once that model is trained,
you have the ability

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.990
to assess that model, optimize
it, and even understand

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.900
the signals behind your
model's predictions.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.990
And you do that
with explainable AI.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:47.460
And explainable AI lets you
dive deeper into your model

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:51.990
and understand which factors
are playing a role in defining

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.360
what that model is predicting.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.890
Once you're happy
with the model,

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.800
you deploy it to an
endpoint to serve it

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.440
for online predictions using
the API or the console.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.940
Now, this deployment includes
all the physical resources

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.820
and the scalable
hardware that's needed

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:15.420
to scale that model for lower
latency and online predictions.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.640
You can, of course, use
the undeployed model

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.410
for batch predictions.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.510
Once the model is
deployed, you can

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.900
get the predictions using
either the command line

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:31.020
interface, or the console
UI, or the SDK and the APIs.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.240
At this point, you
might be wondering

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.460
how this looks in the
console and where to find it.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.870
So let me give you a little
tour of the dashboard.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.660
In the console, when
we click on Vertex AI,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.940
we land on the
dashboard, where you

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.390
can see the recent data
sets, recent models,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.730
and get predictions.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.220
On the left, you have
all the steps that

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.650
are involved in the
machine learning workflow,

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.280
all the way from data
sets to predictions.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.280
In the data sets, you can
create your data sets,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.760
depending on the type of data
and your prediction task.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.390
Supported data types include
image, tabular, text,

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:10.590
or videos.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.770
If your prediction test doesn't
fall into one of these use

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.450
cases, don't worry.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.690
You can still use Vertex AI
for your custom model training

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:19.920
and prediction.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.720
Once you have
created a data set,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.840
you can see it listed
in the data set list.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.940
In the Notebook
section of the console,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:30.360
you can create your
customized notebook instances

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.360
with the type of environment
and GPUs you want.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.090
The Training tab, you can see
and create your training jobs.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:51.570
The beauty is that you
can have one data set,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.120
but you can train it
in different ways.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.540
With AutoML, you can
train a high quality model

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.160
with minimal effort.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.420
AutoML Edge for models that
are optimized for Edge devices.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.490
And with the Custom
Training option,

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:08.820
you can train models
built with any framework,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.880
via pre-built or
custom containers.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.010
Pre-built containers
are available

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:17.790
for supported frameworks
such as TensorFlow, PyTorch,

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:20.640
scikit-learn, and XGBoost.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.760
You provide your code
as a Python package.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.250
The Custom Containers
option allows

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:30.450
you to train models built
with any framework or language

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.690
by putting your training
application code in a Docker

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.420
container, push it to
Container Registry,

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.910
and run training on Vertex.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.760
You can accelerate the
training with GPUs,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:45.540
and also apply
hyperparameter tuning.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.810
In the Models tab, you
can see all the models

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:50.280
you have created.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:54.480
Here, you can also import models
trained outside of Google Cloud

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.840
to serve it for online
and batch predictions.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.260
In order to use a model, you
create an endpoint, which

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.330
brings us to our next step.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.720
Creating an endpoint is
how you serve your models

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.490
for online predictions.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:11.400
Each model can have
multiple endpoints.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.640
Enter the compute resources
so your endpoint can

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.940
auto scale the resources
based on your traffic.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.670
You can even split
traffic across endpoints

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.910
and send model logs
to Cloud Logging.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.900
Once the model is live, we
can make predictions in the UI

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.300
or through the SDK.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.250
In the Batch
Predictions tab, you

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:34.260
can make predictions
on the batch

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.570
of data from Cloud Storage.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:39.730
This was a pretty
high-level overview

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:43.270
where we saw that Vertex AI
provides the tools to support

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.200
your entire machine learning
workflow from data management

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.000
all the way to predictions.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.820
In the next episodes,
we will dive much deeper

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:54.940
into all of these steps and
build end-to-end machine

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:55.870
learning workflow.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:58.270
In the meantime, let's
continue our discussion

00:06:58.270 --> 00:06:59.440
in the comments below.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:02.200
I'm excited to hear
all about your machine

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:04.830
learning use case and workflow.

